  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nAs of this writing there are large cumulonimbus clouds in southwest \nVirginia and southern Kentucky producing showers and a few \nthunderstorms. A few gusty winds are possible with these storms, \npaired with lightning. A change of pace from the absolutely \nsuppressive ridge we've had the last few days that stymied any \nactivity.\n\nSpeaking of the ridge, the heat dome of doom, I mean the large and \nin charge pile of hot air, it is gradually shifting its central axis \nwest towards Arkansas. As a consequence we're getting enough \nmoisture now filtering in to begin sparking a few showers and \nthunderstorms where the oppressiveness and dry air has lessened \nenough. Today that's southern Kentucky and southern Virginia, \nmaybe a spot or two in Tennessee. As we head into the weekend and \nearly next week, and the ridge continues its two step to the left,\nthis area will expand to the whole of the area and everyone will \nhave a shot for a thunderstorm or two. Not everyone will get rain \nthough, we're still in a summer pattern so its still hit or miss \nstyle. Next week we'll look to the Midwestern weather pattern to \nsee if a forecast trough can spark better storm coverage down here\nbetween Monday and Wednesday, primarily during the afternoon \nhours still. For now not expecting severe storms, but any decent \nthunderstorm could pack brief gusty winds inside the core.\n\nTracking Chattanooga's consecutive 90F record, next Wednesday's\nforecast high is 89, and the day after is 87. So perhaps an end is\nin sight for the worst of the heat.",
